{
  "evidenceKind": "deterministic reconstruction from actual request, pinned indexer and unchanged native PrepareText source; not native-buffer capture",
  "normalized": "Before the doors open, a librarian wheels a cart of returned books across the quiet room. Each book has a destination, but that destination is not always the shelf. One is waiting for a reader who reserved it last week. Another belongs to a branch on the other side of town. A third has a torn page that needs a careful repair. The morning begins by sorting these small differences. A barcode makes the work faster. When the librarian scans it, the computer finds the record for that particular copy. The system can mark the book as returned and check whether someone else is waiting. It does not have to guess from the title, because two copies of the same story can be in different places at the same time. By midday, the cart is nearly empty. The reserved book sits behind the desk, ready to be collected. The travelling book is packed for the afternoon delivery. The repaired book is back among its neighbours, where a visitor can discover it without knowing anything about its morning journey. This ordinary routine explains something important about shared resources. A library works because information moves with the objects. Readers see a welcoming room and shelves full of possibilities. Behind that simple experience, a series of small, reliable handoffs keeps each book available to the next person who needs it.",
  "wrapped": "<en>Before the doors open, a librarian wheels a cart of returned books across the quiet room. Each book has a destination, but that destination is not always the shelf. One is waiting for a reader who reserved it last week. Another belongs to a branch on the other side of town. A third has a torn page that needs a careful repair. The morning begins by sorting these small differences. A barcode makes the work faster. When the librarian scans it, the computer finds the record for that particular copy. The system can mark the book as returned and check whether someone else is waiting. It does not have to guess from the title, because two copies of the same story can be in different places at the same time. By midday, the cart is nearly empty. The reserved book sits behind the desk, ready to be collected. The travelling book is packed for the afternoon delivery. The repaired book is back among its neighbours, where a visitor can discover it without knowing anything about its morning journey. This ordinary routine explains something important about shared resources. A library works because information moves with the objects. Readers see a welcoming room and shelves full of possibilities. Behind that simple experience, a series of small, reliable handoffs keeps each book available to the next person who needs it.<\/en>",
